iversal orchestration layer that links heterogeneous platforms, preventing vendor lock-in.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Quantitative Assessment**\n\n| Metric | Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Time to First Actionable Result** | < 2 Weeks | Metrics for \"Time-to-Value\" are the primary KPI for enterprise procurement. |\n| **Circuit Breaker Threshold** | < 5% Variance | Max budget/token deviation allowed before auto-pause is triggered. |\n| **Schema Adaptation Latency** | < 4 Hours | Time taken for a \"Hot-swappable\" tool adapter to adjust to 3rd-party API changes. |\n\n---\n\n### **3. Actionable Startup Stages**\n\n1.  **Validation (Months 0-2):** Validate \"Time to First Actionable Result.\" Focus on \"High-Pain, Low-Risk\" workflows where the cost of human error is low but the volume of repetitive tasks is high (e.g., ticket triage, document summarizing).\n2.  **Infrastructure Hardening (Months 2-5):** Build the **Hot-swappable Tool Adapters** and the **Budgetary Circuit Breaker.** This is the \"Safety-First\" build cycle.\n3.  **Enterprise Pilot (Months 6-9):** Deploy within a sandbox environment. Focus on onboarding one specific department (e.g., Finance Ops) to prove our \"Constraint Engine\" can handle complex business rules without recursive looping.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Technical Requirements & Recommendations**\n\n1.  **The \"Circuit Breaker\" Protocol (TRD Addition):** The Orchestration Layer must implement a mandatory **Spend-Cap & Loop-Limit**. If an agent enters a recursive loop or exceeds 30% of its budget for a specific task, the system must trigger an automatic \"State Freeze\" and notify a human supervisor. This is non-negotiable for enterprise risk mitigation.\n2.  **Hot-swappable Tool Adapters:** To mitigate \"Integration Debt\" (e.g., a Salesforce API update breaking our connector), we must build a **Modular Proxy Layer**. This allows our engineers to swap out individual tool adapters without touching the core orchestration logic, ensuring 