s moving away from pure token consumption (which creates unpredictable enterprise costs) toward **outcome-based pricing** (e.g., successful task completion), which aligns NEXUS\u2019s revenue with the customer's ROI.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Quantitative Assessment**\n\n| Metric | Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Task Success Rate** | >85% | Enterprise adoption fails if human oversight exceeds 15% of task time. |\n| **Context Compression** | 10x-50x | Mandatory to manage token costs and latency in hierarchical RAG systems. |\n| **Human-in-the-Loop (HITL)** | 100% for Critical Tasks | Required for regulatory/security compliance (financial/PII). |\n\n---\n\n### **3. Actionable Startup Stages**\n\n1.  **Validation (Months 0-2):** Validate \"Time Saved\" vs \"Accuracy\" via 15 discovery interviews. *Crucial:* Do not build until you confirm if the customer is buying an *assistant* (time-saver) or an *executor* (accuracy-focused).\n2.  **Modular Alpha (Months 2-5):** Build the \"Orchestrator\" and \"Memory-Store\" (Postgres + Vector DB). Use the MVP to prove \"Self-Correction\" test suites in a sandbox.\n3.  **Enterprise Pilot (Months 6-9):** Roll out to a controlled group of mid-managers using the \"Constraint Engine\" as the primary selling point to gain security clearance.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Recommendations for NEXUS AI**\n\n1.  **Prioritize the \"Orchestrator\" TRD:** Do not proceed with the Business Model Canvas until the Technical Requirements Document (TRD) for the Orchestration Layer is complete. The business model depends entirely on whether your architecture can handle *asynchronous, multi-step agent delegation* reliably.\n2.  **Define \"Agent-as-a-Service\" Pricing Early:** Based on the shift in 2025 market trends, avoid standard token-based pricing. Propose a **Usage-based Subscription model** (e.g., \"Cost per 100 successful task reconciliations\") to provide the predictability enterprises demand.\n3.  **Data Gap Alert:** We currently lack a compet