hting our **decoupled orchestration** (Memory-Logic-API separation).\n6.  **Economic Model:** Determine SaaS pricing (Tiered subscription vs. Token consumption) and projected CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) vs. LTV.\n\n#### **Phase 3: Technical Architecture Requirements**\n7.  **Orchestration Layer Development:** Build the core controller responsible for task decomposition, sub-agent delegation, and loop detection.\n8.  **Persistent Memory Module:** Implement a vector database (e.g., Pinecone or Milvus) integrated with a relational DB (PostgreSQL) to store long-term user/enterprise context (similar to the Hestabit memory update implementation).\n9.  **Tooling & API Gateway:** Build standardized wrappers (Toolkits) for third-party integrations (Jira, Slack, Salesforce, GitHub) to ensure agents have \"hands\" to interact with the environment.\n10. **Security & Governance Layer:** Develop an authentication and audit-trail system to ensure agent actions are logs-compliant and user-supervised (Human-in-the-loop).\n\n#### **Phase 4: Implementation (The Build)**\n11. **MVP Scaffolding:** Use a monorepo structure (similar to the Vibhav Food Delivery Backend) to manage microservices for `Orchestrator`, `Agent-Node`, `Memory-Store`, and `API-Gateway`.\n12. **Agent Role Definition:** Develop standard schemas for specialized agents (Researcher, Coder, Auditor) to ensure modularity.\n13. **Testing Pipeline:** Implement \"Self-Correction\" test suites where an agent attempts to fix its own syntax or logic errors using a sandbox environment.\n\n#### **Phase 5: Validation & Go-to-Market**\n14. **Alpha Launch:** Deploy to a closed pilot group (target: professional workflows).\n15. **Performance Monitoring:** Implement a real-time dashboard to track \"Agent Hallucination Rate\" and \"Task Success Rate.\"\n16. **Iterative Feedback Loop:** Use agent telemetry to identify which tools or workflows users trigger most frequently.\n\n---\n\n### **Potential Failure Points & Mitigation**\n\n*